================================================================================
COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS / 常见误区
================================================================================

  "Apache-2.0 is stricter than MIT, so no commercial use."
    FALSE. Apache-2.0 explicitly permits commercial use, closed-source
    distribution, and resale. It restricts nothing except attribution.

  "Renaming variables avoids the attribution requirement."
    FALSE. The obligation attaches to the Derivative Work, not to identifiers.
    Renaming while hiding the origin is itself a violation.

  "Internal use still requires attribution."
    FALSE. Section 4 triggers only on distribution. Private use creates no
    obligation. This is the true boundary; we do not overstate it.

  "Trained weights must be open-sourced."
    FALSE. Apache-2.0 has no copyleft clause. Weights, data, and your new code
    may all remain closed.

  "Citing LensKD means I no longer need to cite DSKD."
    FALSE. Both are required, independently. LensKD is an incremental
    improvement over DSKD, not a replacement.

  "MIT already requires attribution."
    PARTLY FALSE. MIT requires retaining the copyright notice, but does NOT
    require stating the source in documentation or papers, and does NOT
    require marking modified files. That gap is why this project migrated.

